WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.600
Welcome to RefMatch Evaluation Lab, a project by Mohammed Roqa.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.505
This walkthrough explores the problem, runs an evaluation, and follows a failure

00:00:08.565 --> 00:00:09.920
back to its evidence.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.940
A confident explanation can hide an unsafe referee allocation.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.420
A late withdrawal can invalidate a plan, while uncertain attendance should prevent

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.935
clearance. RefMatch tests whether an AI can read changing committee evidence, apply

00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:26.150
appointment rules, and recognize missing information.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.410
The rules and cases are experimental.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.390
Today's demonstration uses clearly labelled simulations.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:38.410
First, check the reference answers. All 136 authored references agree with the deterministic

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:43.625
validator. The corpus has 24 families, with 92 development cases and 44

00:00:43.685 --> 00:00:47.535
public validation cases. Related variants stay together.

00:00:47.595 --> 00:00:50.175
Sixteen cases test narrative evidence reading.

00:00:50.235 --> 00:00:54.080
Both splits are public, so validation is not an unseen benchmark.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.410
Reference agreement checks consistency, not expert approval.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.435
Two tasks separate the source of a failure.

00:01:01.495 --> 00:01:04.900
Rule audit supplies structured facts and tests rule application.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.525
Evidence reading withholds reference committee facts and asks for extracted statuses,

00:01:08.585 --> 00:01:13.110
source citations, and a verdict. A demonstrated violation means invalid, even if

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.650
another check is unresolved. With no established violation, an unresolved required

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.920
check means insufficient information.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.660
Only cleared checks permit valid. Scoring compares verdicts, rule categories, extracted

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.390
facts, and citations against references.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.920
Explanatory prose is left for human review.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.460
Let's choose Evidence reading, Public validation, and Overconfident clearance.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.925
We keep one trial and run the simulation.

00:01:37.985 --> 00:01:40.390
This task and split contain eight cases.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.120
Each batch freezes its case list, prompt version, settings, and dataset and rule

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.080
hashes. No model provider is called.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.220
All eight cases completed, with no errors or unrun cases.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.675
Five verdicts are correct: 62.5 percent end to end accuracy.

00:01:56.735 --> 00:02:00.640
But three of the five invalid or unresolved plans were incorrectly cleared: 60 percent

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.960
unsafe clearance. That includes two of three unresolved cases and one of two invalid

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.960
plans. These denominators matter. Accuracy includes every planned case,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.300
while conditional safety rates must be read alongside coverage.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.615
These are simulated results, not measured model performance.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.485
Filter for unsafe clearances and inspect this attendance case.

00:02:21.545 --> 00:02:25.835
The earlier roster says available. The latest usable note says travel is still unconfirmed

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:28.530
and explicitly warns against assuming attendance.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:32.050
A later note falls after the planning cutoff, and the untrusted attachment is not

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.025
a committee update. The correct verdict is insufficient information.

00:02:36.085 --> 00:02:39.435
The scripted response instead extracts available and returns valid.

00:02:39.495 --> 00:02:43.075
The stage diagnosis shows how a wrong extracted fact produces the wrong downstream

00:02:43.135 --> 00:02:43.995
decision.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.770
A second failure is more direct. Both coordinators confirm that the referee has withdrawn

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.910
and is unavailable. The reference therefore marks the allocation invalid.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.760
The overconfident simulation still calls it valid.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.005
Together, these examples distinguish an unresolved check from a demonstrated violation,

00:03:01.065 --> 00:03:03.385
and show why either one should prevent clearance.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.215
Now run Reference assisted answers on the same task and split.

00:03:08.275 --> 00:03:12.485
The simulation matches all eight references and clears none of the five unsafe plans.

00:03:12.545 --> 00:03:16.020
Extraction and citation matches also reach one hundred percent.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.190
This is expected because the simulation reads the answer key.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:23.355
It verifies the scoring pipeline; it does not demonstrate model intelligence.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:29.110
Download the batch report to preserve the frozen settings, input hashes, and individual

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:33.750
attempts. Scores can also be exported as a spreadsheet friendly CSV.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.045
Blind review packs omit authored answers and revealing titles.

00:03:37.105 --> 00:03:40.760
Separate domain and technical reviewers can record judgments, with a third adjudicator

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.985
for disagreements. Reviewer identities and expertise are self declared.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.540
The failure explorer makes two patterns visible: uncertainty promoted to fact, and

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.100
a withdrawal ignored. Both lead to unsafe clearance.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.355
These runs are scripted simulations.

00:03:56.415 --> 00:04:00.170
For frontier model evaluation, configure an exact model through the local provider

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.920
runner and use the same frozen batches and scoring.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.860
Then inspect each measured failure against its source evidence.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.595
RefMatch. Find the failure. Follow the evidence.

